Unreleased
    * On its first run, a menu offers to write a config.toml with the
      recommended settings, or with a personal plugin
    * `plugin add`: adds a plugin (a GitHub repository, git or file
      URL, or path) to `config.toml`, after asking, and fetches it
    * Plugins can add commands: `sh::builtin(name, fn)` registers a
      built-in written in Rhai, and `sh::read_line()` reads its input
    * `type` names the plugin that added a built-in
    * std.completion completes about 160 more commands: luish, sh, bash
      and zsh, find, sed, awk, jq, rg, less, compressors, systemctl,
      journalctl, loginctl, ps, top, mount, tmux, curl, wget, ip, gpg,
      openssl, cargo, rustup, go, gcc, cmake, gdb, python, vim, nano, pip,
      uv, conda, pixi, npm, yarn, pnpm, apt, dpkg, dnf, rpm, pacman,
      zypper, apk, brew, snap, flatpak ...
    * std.completion asks Cobra programs (gh, docker, kubectl ...) and nix
      for their completions; `complete-cobra PROG...` adds more programs
    * std.git-completion is now part of std.completion
    * `import` in an extension's module is relative to that module's file

Version 0.2.0 2026-09-29 by luispedro
    * Fix crashes on Tab after a word like `--opt=value`, and on a plugin
      source with an empty path
    * zsh's parameter flags: `${(j:,:)a[@]}`, `${(s:,:)x}`, `${(o)a[@]}`,
      `${(k)h[@]}`, and `@ v f F L U C u O i n a`
    * `${!x}` (bash): indirection, also with an operator (`${!x:-d}`)
      and to elements (`x='a[1]'`); `${!prefix@}` lists variable names
    * A lone `"${!a[@]}"` on an empty array gives no word, as `"$@"`
    * Tab after `${a[` completes the array's indices (in numeric order)
      or keys
    * `\]` is a `]` in the key of `${h[...]}`, as in `h[...]=v`
    * Plugins read and set arrays: `sh::getarray`, `sh::getmap`, and
      `sh::setvar` with an array or a map
    * `typeset -f` prints functions, `typeset +f` their names
    * `typeset -l` and `-u` (lower and upper case), and `typeset -U` (zsh:
      unique arrays, as in `typeset -U path`)
    * `typeset -i`: integer variables, as in zsh and bash
    * `typeset -p` prints special variables (`path`, `pipestatus`, `UID`)
    * `dirstack` (zsh): the directory stack as an array, which
      `dirstack=(...)` sets
    * `path` (zsh): the directories of `PATH` as an array, which
      `path=(...)` and `path+=(...)` set
    * Special variables such as `UID` are special again after a function
      with `local UID` returns, or after `UID=x cmd`
    * `[[ =~ ]]` sets `match`, `mbegin` and `mend` (zsh) and `BASH_REMATCH`
      (bash)
    * `pipestatus` (zsh) and `PIPESTATUS` (bash): the statuses of the
      last pipeline's commands
    * `${!a[@]}` gives the indices or keys of an array, as in bash
    * Associative arrays (`typeset -A`), as in zsh and bash
    * `a=([i]=x)` assigns to an index, as in zsh and bash
    * `typeset` and `declare`, as in zsh and bash
    * `read -A` (zsh) and `read -a NAME` (bash) read into an array
    * An empty array reads as unset, as in zsh and bash
    * Arrays, as in zsh and bash: `a=(x y)`, `${a[i]}`, `"${a[@]}"`, `a+=(z)`
    * `NAME+=value` appends to a variable
    * `${x:offset:length}` and `${x/pattern/replacement}`, as in zsh and bash
    * Syntax highlighting shows unset variables in a different colour
    * `[[ ... ]]` conditions, as in zsh and bash
    * `function NAME { ... }` definitions, as in zsh and bash
    * `__luish_internal check-cache` reruns the startup files to check
      their caches, which record when they were built
    * zsh's `RANDOM`, `SECONDS`, `EPOCHSECONDS`, `EPOCHREALTIME`, `UID`,
      `EUID`, `GID`, `EGID` and `HISTCMD`
    * `SHLVL` is maintained
    * `set -o pipefail`
    * `let` built-in, as in zsh
    * `shopt` (not found) suggests the matching `setopt`
    * `builtin` built-in, as in zsh and bash
    * Tab expands globs, variables and substitutions, as in zsh
    * Don't print git's error for a locked plugin commit that isn't fetched yet
    * Fix a crash on Tab after a stray `)`

Version 0.1.0 2026-09-27 by luispedro
    * First release
    * POSIX sh with dash's behaviour where POSIX is ambiguous, plus `local`
    * As fast as dash on scripts (faster on arithmetic and function calls)
    * Interactive use: zsh-style keys, syntax highlighting, autosuggestions,
      completion menu, shared history in zsh's format, zsh prompts and glob
      qualifiers, job control
    * Plugins in shell and Rhai, fetched from git and pinned in a lock file
    * Cached startup files for instant startup
    * Configuration in config.toml
    * Binaries for Linux on x86_64 and aarch64 (glibc and static musl)
